.TH MAIL_FILTER.CONF 5 "August 2026" "mail_filter 1.0.0" "File Formats"
.nh
.SH NAME
mail_filter.conf \- configuration file for mail_filter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.TP 40
.I /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/mail_filter.conf
FreeBSD
.TP
.I /etc/mail_filter/mail_filter.conf
Linux
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mail_filter.conf
is the main configuration file for
.BR mail_filter (8).
The file uses INI syntax. Section names are enclosed in brackets and keys are
assigned with
.BR = .
Lines beginning with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments.
.PP
mail_filter can optionally send a structured message log through
.BR syslog (3)
with the
.B mail
facility. This output is intended for sites that want selected decoded message
metadata in the normal Postfix mail log without running another milter.
It is disabled by default and is independent of the ordinary mail_filter
application log.
.PP
The configuration file is read at process startup. SIGUSR1 reloads only the
contents of the rules and whitelist files. Changes to this file require a
service restart.
.PP
This file, along with
.B mail_filter_rules.conf
and
.BR mail_filter_whitelist.cidr ,
is created once from a packaged template the first time the package is
installed (or after the file has been deleted) and is never touched again
by an upgrade -- see
.BR mail_filter (8)
for how the install scripts pick its initial
.B [milter]
.B socket
value. On Debian/Ubuntu these are deliberately not dpkg conffiles, so
.B apt remove
leaves the whole
.I /etc/mail_filter
directory in place, while
.B apt purge
deletes it.
.SH SECTIONS AND KEYS
.SS [milter]
.TP
.B socket = \fIendpoint\fR
Unix-domain milter endpoint. The default shipped in the sample config
works when Postfix's
.B smtpd
is not chrooted (check with
.BR "postconf -M" ;
a
.B y
in the chroot column means it is chrooted -- FreeBSD's default Postfix is
not, Debian/Ubuntu's often is):
.PP
.nf
.RS
socket = unix:/var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.sock
.RE
.fi
.PP
If
.B smtpd
.B is
chrooted, it can only reach a milter socket that physically exists under
its own
.B queue_directory
(see
.BR "postconf -h queue_directory" ,
typically
.IR /var/spool/postfix ).
Use Postfix's own
.B private/
convention instead, on both sides:
.PP
.nf
.RS
socket = unix:/var/spool/postfix/private/mail_filter.sock
.RE
.fi
.PP
with
.B "smtpd_milters = unix:private/mail_filter.sock"
in
.BR main.cf .
If this file is being created fresh by the install (first install, or the
file was deleted) and Postfix's
.B smtpd_milters
or
.B non_smtpd_milters
already references this socket by name, the FreeBSD and Debian post-install
scripts resolve the correct path themselves \(em following the
.B private/
convention through Postfix's own
.B queue_directory
when used \(em and write it into the new file directly; Postfix's own
directives are trusted as already correct and are never modified. If an
existing file is preserved instead, or Postfix is not yet configured with a
matching milter directive to resolve the path from, the scripts fall back to
detecting a chrooted
.B smtpd
or
.B cleanup
whose
.B socket
does not resolve under its
.B queue_directory
and print this exact fix; that fallback check is purely informational and
never modifies Postfix's configuration or this file.
.TP
.B timeout = \fIseconds\fR
Milter timeout. Default: 600.
.TP
.B loglevel = DEBUG|INFO|WARNING|ERROR
Controls the ordinary application log configured by
.BR logfile .
It does not enable, disable, or filter the structured
.B [mail_log]
output.
.TP
.B log_timestamp = bsd|iso8601|\fIstrftime-format\fR
Selects the timestamp format used only in the ordinary application log
configured by
.BR logfile .
The default is
.B bsd
on FreeBSD and
.B iso8601
on Linux/Debian, matching each platform's native syslog convention.
The
.B bsd
value preserves the historical format, for example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
Aug 28 18:50:23 mail_filter[1234] WARNING ...
.RE
.fi
.PP
The
.B iso8601
value writes an offset-aware local timestamp with microsecond precision, for
example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
2026-08-28T18:50:23.652662+00:00 mail_filter[1234] WARNING ...
.RE
.fi
.PP
Any other value is used verbatim as a format string for Python's
.I time.strftime()
\(em see
.UR https://docs.python.org/3/library/time.html#time.strftime
the Python documentation
.UE
for the full list of directives.
Directives are case-sensitive
.RI ( %Y
is a 4-digit year,
.I %y
a 2-digit one). For example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
log_timestamp = %Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S
.RE
.fi
.PP
writes
.PP
.nf
.RS
2026-08-28 18:50:23 mail_filter[1234] WARNING ...
.RE
.fi
.PP
.I %f
(sub-second digits) is
.B not
supported in a custom format, even though it exists on Python's
.I datetime.strftime()
\(em use the
.B iso8601
preset instead for microsecond precision.
An unrecognized directive is not always rejected at startup; verify the
result with
.B mail_filter --configtest
or by checking the log itself.
.PP
This option does not control timestamps in
.BR [mail_log] .
Structured mail-log records are sent through syslog(3), and their timestamp
is added and formatted by the system logger such as rsyslog or syslog-ng.
Therefore matching the two log files is an administrator choice rather than
automatic detection by mail_filter. For example, on an Ubuntu host whose
mail.log uses RFC 3339/ISO-style timestamps, setting
.B log_timestamp = iso8601
makes /var/log/mail_filter.log visually consistent with that log.
Changing this option requires a service restart.
.TP
.B max_body_kb = \fIkilobytes\fR
Maximum amount of the message body retained for filtering by the
.B body
test (see
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5)).
Body data received beyond this limit is discarded; the retained
(truncated) prefix is still evaluated against
.B body
rules. A value of
.B 0
disables the limit entirely, which is not recommended on an
Internet-facing MX since it allows unbounded memory use per connection.
Default:
.BR 512 .
This setting has no effect on
.BR subject ,
.BR envelope_from ,
.BR envelope_to ,
.B header
or other non-body tests.
.TP
.B log_match_max_chars = \fIcharacters\fR
In the
.B matched=
field of a REJECT/ACCEPT/warn log line, a regex rule shows the text that
actually matched -- like
.BR pcre2grep (1)'s
.BR \-\-only\-matching ,
e.g. the one emoji character that tripped an emoji rule -- rather than the
regex itself, which for a large character class or alternation list would
otherwise dwarf the rest of the log line.
.B log_match_max_chars
caps how many characters of that matched text are shown before it is cut
off with
.RB \[dq]\ ...\[dq].
A value of
.B 0
means no limit. Default:
.BR 80 .
This setting has no effect on
.BR "mail_filter --test" ,
.BR --test-eml ,
or
.BR --configtest ,
which always print the full matching rule.
.TP
.B add_warn_header = yes|no
If
.BR yes ,
a
.B warn
rule (see
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5))
adds an
.B X-Mail-Filter-Status
header to the delivered message, in the spirit of SpamAssassin's
.BR X-Spam-Status .
A single warn produces:
.PP
.nf
.RS
X-Mail-Filter-Status: WARN "Possible BEC -- bank account change notification"
.RE
.fi
.PP
More than one warn on the same message folds onto one tab-indented,
quoted line per warn, so grepping for one warn's text still finds exactly
one line -- the same principle as SpamAssassin's multi-line
.BR X-Spam-Status :
.PP
.nf
.RS
X-Mail-Filter-Status: WARN
	"Possible BEC -- bank account change notification"
	"Emoji not allowed"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Each warn's text is RFC 2047-encoded automatically if it contains
non-ASCII characters. The header is never added for a
.B REJECT
(the message is never delivered) or when no warn fired
.RI ( ACCEPT
status). Default:
.BR no ,
since unlike every other setting in this section, it changes the
delivered message rather than only logging. Changing this option requires
a service restart -- it toggles a milter capability flag registered at
startup.
.PP
The header is plain text a mail filter downstream of delivery can act on
\(em for example, a Sieve (Pigeonhole) rule that sends security a copy of
anything flagged:
.PP
.nf
.RS
if header :contains "X-Mail-Filter-Status" "WARN" {
    redirect :copy "security@your-domain.foo";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS [files]
.TP
.B rules = \fIpath\fR
Rules file. A relative path is resolved relative to this configuration file.
.TP
.B whitelist = \fIpath\fR
IP/CIDR whitelist file. A relative path is resolved relative to this
configuration file.
.TP
.B logfile = \fIpath\fR
Application log file. Default:
.IR /var/log/mail_filter.log .
This file contains mail_filter operational and decision logging and is
separate from the optional mail-facility syslog record.
.SS [mail_log]
The
.B [mail_log]
section controls one optional structured syslog record per completed message.
The facility is always
.B mail
so that the record follows the system's normal mail logging route. On typical
Ubuntu systems this is
.IR /var/log/mail.log ;
on typical FreeBSD systems it is
.IR /var/log/maillog .
The exact destination is controlled by the local syslog or rsyslog
configuration.
.SS What the logging records
Both
.I logfile
and
.B [mail_log]
write the decoded Subject line and the envelope addresses of real messages,
and keep them for as long as log rotation does. In most jurisdictions and
under most company policies that is personal data.
.PP
Enable the record if your site's rules on retaining message metadata allow
it, and decide deliberately what it is kept for, who may read the file, and
how long it lives. On most mail servers a record of this kind already exists
for operational reasons \(em answering \(lqdid they really send it, and
when\(rq long after the fact \(em and this one is no different in kind.
That decision belongs to the site rather than to this software, and the
answer differs by country, by industry and by employer.
.PP
How much is recorded is set by
.BR format .
Every field is optional: a record of
.B {qid}
and
.B {result}
alone says that a message was filtered without saying anything about who
sent what. The same applies to
.B loglevel
in
.BR [milter] :
.B WARNING
records rejected mail only, where
.B INFO
records every message that passes through.
.TP
.B enabled = yes|no
Enable or disable structured mail-facility logging. Default:
.BR no .
.TP
.B priority = \fIlevel\fR
Syslog priority used for every structured message record. Accepted values are
.BR emerg ,
.BR alert ,
.BR crit ,
.BR err ,
.BR error ,
.BR warning ,
.BR warn ,
.BR notice ,
.BR info ,
and
.BR debug .
Default:
.BR warning .
.TP
.B tag = \fItext\fR
Syslog identifier. Default:
.BR postfix/subject .
With the default tag, a normal syslog prefix resembles:
.PP
.nf
.RS
Aug 28 13:40:15 backup postfix/subject[1234]: ...
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B format = \fItemplate\fR
Template used to build the structured
.B [mail_log]
record. Field references such as
.B {qid},
.B {subject_decoded},
.B {envelope_from},
and
.B {header_from}
are replaced with values from the current message. Literal braces are written
as doubled braces.
.PP
The sequence
.B \et
written directly in the configured template inserts a real horizontal tab.
This is useful for tab-separated records and machine parsing. The sequences
.B \er
and
.B \en
written in the template are not allowed to split one syslog record; they are
emitted as visible escaped text instead.
.PP
Before field substitution, carriage return, line feed, tab, and backslash
characters originating inside message values are converted to visible escaped
text. Therefore an untrusted Subject or other header value cannot introduce a
new record, create an extra tab-separated field, or otherwise alter the field
boundaries defined by the administrator.
.PP
The default template is:
.PP
.nf
.RS
format = {qid}:\\theader Subject: '{subject_decoded}'\\teFrom: '{envelope_from}'\\thFrom: '{header_from}'\\tbody_size={body_size_bytes}\\tentity_count={entity_count}
.RE
.fi
.PP
Available fields are:
.TP 22
.B {qid}
Postfix queue ID from the milter
.B {i}
macro.
.TP
.B {message_id}
Message-ID header, or a dash when absent.
.TP
.B {client_ip}
SMTP client IP address seen by the milter.
.TP
.B {envelope_from}
SMTP MAIL FROM value.
.TP
.B {envelope_to}
Comma-separated SMTP RCPT TO values.
.TP
.B {header_from}
Decoded and unfolded From header value or values.
.TP
.B {header_from_raw}
Original From header value or values as supplied to the milter.
.TP
.B {header_to}
Decoded and unfolded To header value or values.
.TP
.B {header_to_raw}
Original To header value or values as supplied to the milter.
.TP
.B {header_cc}
Decoded and unfolded Cc header value or values.
.TP
.B {header_cc_raw}
Original Cc header value or values as supplied to the milter.
.TP
.B {reply_to}
Decoded and unfolded Reply-To header value or values.
.TP
.B {reply_to_raw}
Original Reply-To header value or values as supplied to the milter.
.TP
.B {x_original_to}
X-Original-To header value or values.
.TP
.B {delivered_to}
Delivered-To header value or values.
.TP
.B {subject_raw}
Raw Subject header value as supplied to the milter.
.TP
.B {subject_decoded}
Subject after MIME encoded-word decoding.
.TP
.B {decode_error}
Subject decoding error text, or a dash when no error occurred.
.TP
.B {body_size}
.TQ
.B {body_size_bytes}
Number of bytes of message body retained for filtering (after any
.B max_body_kb
truncation). Both fields are synonyms with the same value.
.TP
.B {body_truncated}
.B yes
if the body was cut off at
.BR max_body_kb ,
.B no
otherwise.
.TP
.B {entity_count}
Count of HTML-entity-style sequences
.RI ( &#123; ", " &#x7B; ", " &amp; )
found in the retained body, after QP soft-break merging. A high count on
a plain-text or lightly formatted message is a common obfuscation
signal; see
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5)
SECTION 3 of the sample rules file for an example rule that uses it.
.TP
.B {result}
Final mail_filter decision, normally ACCEPT or REJECT.
.TP
.B {reason}
Decision reason. For a rejection this is the configured rejection text; for an
accepted message it can be whitelisted, no_rule_matched, rule, or
fallthrough.
.TP
.B {warn}
WARN action text, or a dash.
.TP
.B {warn_rules}
Rule and regex information associated with WARN actions, or a dash.
.TP
.B {rule_line}
Matched rule-line path, or a dash.
.TP
.B {matched}
Matched regex details, or a dash.
.SH FORMAT VALIDATION
.B mail_filter --configtest
validates the
.B [mail_log]
section in addition to the rules and whitelist. It rejects invalid boolean
values, unknown priorities, unknown field names, conversion operators, and
format specifications. When mail logging is enabled, the template must contain
at least one field reference.
.PP
For example, this is invalid:
.PP
.nf
.RS
format = {qid}\\t{unknown_field}
.RE
.fi
.PP
and causes
.B --configtest
to return a non-zero status.

.SS MULTIPLE RECORDS WITH CONTENT-FILTER REINJECTION
The
.B [mail_log]
facility writes one record each time a message completes a mail_filter
transaction. It does not attempt to identify two Postfix queue entries as the
same logical message.
.PP
When Postfix sends a message to an external content filter and the filtered
message is then reinjected into Postfix, mail_filter can therefore see the
same logical message more than once. Each Postfix pass has its own queue ID and
produces its own structured mail-log record. This is expected behavior and is
not duplicate logging by mail_filter.
.PP
For example, a common SpamAssassin pipe configuration uses:
.PP
.nf
.RS
smtp      inet  n       -       y       -       -       smtpd
  -o content_filter=spamassassin

spamassassin unix -     n       n       -       -       pipe
  user=debian-spamd argv=/usr/bin/spamc -f -e
  /usr/sbin/sendmail -oi -f ${sender} ${recipient}
.RE
.fi
.PP
The first record is produced for the message received by smtpd. After
.B spamc
returns the filtered message through
.BR sendmail (1),
Postfix accepts it again through the local submission path, assigns a new queue
ID, and mail_filter processes it again. Two records with the same
Message-ID but different queue IDs are therefore normal in this topology.
.PP
If only one mail_filter pass is desired, the Postfix content-filter topology
must be changed so that the reinjected path does not invoke the same milter.
That is a Postfix configuration decision; mail_filter does not suppress the
second record because it represents a real second milter transaction.


.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Backup MX with human-readable labeled fields
.nf
.RS
[mail_log]
enabled  = yes
priority = warning
tag      = postfix/subject
format   = {qid}:\\theader Subject: '{subject_decoded}'\\teFrom: '{envelope_from}'\\thFrom: '{header_from}'\\tbody_size={body_size_bytes}\\tentity_count={entity_count}
.RE
.fi
.SS Tab-separated audit record
.nf
.RS
[mail_log]
enabled  = yes
priority = warning
tag      = postfix/subject
format   = {qid}\\tMessageId={message_id}\\traw_SUBJECT="{subject_raw}"\\tdecoded_SUBJECT="{subject_decoded}"\\teFrom="{envelope_from}"\\thFrom="{header_from}"\\tbody_size={body_size_bytes}\\tentity_count={entity_count}
.RE
.fi
.SH UBUNTU RSYSLOG AND TAB CHARACTERS
On Ubuntu, rsyslog normally escapes a horizontal tab received through syslog
as the visible sequence
.B #011
when writing the message to
.IR /var/log/mail.log .
If the
.B [mail_log]
template uses
.B \\t
as a machine-readable field separator and real tab characters are required in
the file, create for example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
/etc/rsyslog.d/10-tabs.conf
.RE
.fi
.PP
with this content:
.PP
.nf
.RS
global(parser.escapeControlCharacterTab="off")
.RE
.fi
.PP
Validate the rsyslog configuration and restart the service:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rsyslogd -N1
systemctl restart rsyslog
.RE
.fi
.PP
This setting disables escaping for TAB only. Other control characters continue
to use rsyslog's normal escaping behavior. Header-originated TAB, CR, and LF
characters are still escaped by mail_filter before template substitution, so
they cannot create extra structured fields or physical log lines.
.SH RELOAD AND RESTART
SIGUSR1 reloads the contents of the rules and whitelist files only.
Any change to
.BR mail_filter.conf ,
including
.B [mail_log]
settings, requires a full service restart.
.PP
FreeBSD:
.PP
.nf
.RS
service mail_filter configtest
service mail_filter restart
.RE
.fi
.PP
Linux:
.PP
.nf
.RS
mail_filter --configtest
systemctl restart mail_filter
.RE
.fi
